module sysl

/** What a subscript means on a type that has no elements of its own.
 *
 * A built-in array or slice is indexed by walking to an address, and that is the compiler's; these
 * two are what `b[i]` means when the receiver is a type someone wrote. The difference is not
 * cosmetic -- reading through `Index` is a **call**, so it yields a value rather than a place, which
 * is why `b[i] += 1` is refused rather than quietly evaluating the receiver and the index twice.
 *
 * Reading and writing are separate traits because a type may offer one without the other: a view
 * that computes its elements has an `Index` and no `IndexSet` to give.
 */
trait Index[I, E]
    index(self, i: I) -> E

trait IndexSet[I, E]
    index_set(*self, i: I, v: E)

/** What makes a subscript on a type someone wrote a **place**, as an array's element is.
 *
 * `index_place` answers the address of the element, checked against the bounds, and `b[i]` means
 * `*b.index_place(i)` -- so `b[i].f` reads one field without copying the element, `b[i].f = v` and
 * `b[i] += 1` write the element where it lives, and `ref e = b[i]` names it. A type that has this
 * is indexed through it rather than through `Index` and `IndexSet`, which it may still implement for
 * code generic over them.
 *
 * The trait takes `*self`, so a container whose elements are its own bytes answers an address in
 * the container rather than in a copy of it; a handle whose elements live behind it -- a `Buf` --
 * takes `self`, which an implementation may. The address is good until the storage moves, and a
 * member that can move it is marked `@relocates`, which is what keeps a live `ref` to an element
 * from seeing the call.
 */
trait IndexPlace[I, E]
    index_place(*self, i: I) -> *E

    /** What `b[i]` reads through inside an `@unchecked` block: the same address, with the bounds
     * test left out where the container knows how to leave it out (`reference/arrays.md § Unchecked
     * access`). **The default is the checked one**, so a container that has not said how keeps its
     * test inside the block too -- skipping a check is something a container opts into, never
     * something it is opted into by a block it cannot see.
     */
    index_place_unchecked(*self, i: I) -> *E = self.index_place(i)

    /** The element as a view of one, **carrying the owner of the storage it lives in** -- what a view
     * of part of the element (`c[i][..]`, `c[i].cells[1..]`) is taken through, so that the view keeps
     * that storage alive past a growth or a drop of the container, as a view of part of a `Buf`'s
     * element does. A container whose elements live in storage a view owns -- a `Buf`, a `[]T` --
     * answers a one-element part of that view; **the default answers one whose owner is nothing**,
     * which is right for elements in the container's own bytes or in static storage, and is the `*T`
     * tier for anything else.
     */
    index_view(*self, i: I) -> []E = self.index_place(i)[0..<1]

    /** `index_view` inside an `@unchecked` block, as `index_place_unchecked` is `index_place` there. */
    index_view_unchecked(*self, i: I) -> []E = self.index_place_unchecked(i)[0..<1]

/** What a `for` walks, when what it walks is not a range, an array or a slice.
 *
 * The cursor is advanced through `*self`, so a loop iterates a *copy* of whatever it was given --
 * which is why a cursor that means to report something after the walk (a reader's failure latch)
 * has to borrow what it reports on rather than own it.
 *
 * `next` answers with an `Option`, so ending and yielding are one question with one answer, and
 * there is no separate `has_next` for the two to disagree about.
 *
 * The element is an **associated** type rather than a parameter, because a cursor walks one kind of
 * thing and the choice is the cursor's. That is what lets a signature be generic over what it walks
 * -- `count_all[I: Iterate](it: I)` names no element and needs none, where `Iterate[E]` would have
 * asked for an `E` nothing in the call could say. An object still says which: `*Iterate[string]`.
 */
trait Iterate
    type Item
    next(*self) -> Option[Self::Item]

/** What a `for` walks a **container** through: `for x in c` over a type implementing this is
 * `for x in c.walk()`, the sequence asked for once before the first turn.
 *
 * A container is deliberately not a cursor itself -- a `Buf`'s elements already sit in memory, and
 * reading them by index costs an index where a cursor would cost a call apiece -- so `walk` answers
 * with whatever is cheapest to walk: a slice for storage that is contiguous, a cursor for storage
 * that is not. What it answers is what the loop walks, element type and all, so `for (k, v) in m`
 * over a `Map` takes apart the pairs its cursor yields.
 *
 * The sequence is a **snapshot** in the sense its type gives it: a slice is the elements there when
 * the loop began, so an element pushed during the walk is not visited.
 */
trait Walk
    type Walker
    walk(self) -> Self::Walker

/** What a value that can be called looks like, one trait per arity.
 *
 * There is one per arity rather than one variadic trait because a call's argument types are part of
 * what it promises, and there is no way to write that variadically. `call` takes `*self` so that a
 * closure may carry mutable state -- a counter that answers differently each time is a thing worth
 * being able to write.
 */
trait Fn0[R]
    call(*self) -> R

trait Fn1[A, R]
    call(*self, a: A) -> R

trait Fn2[A, B, R]
    call(*self, a: A, b: B) -> R

trait Fn3[A, B, C, R]
    call(*self, a: A, b: B, c: C) -> R

trait Fn4[A, B, C, D, R]
    call(*self, a: A, b: B, c: C, d: D) -> R
